#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "cb31a55d-6107-509a-80e2-9bdffcaff6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5590" :question/problem "![](images/16/169c756c5420efe2234b3f20da794cc77e45f24c27cfafadfdf899ea9330c986.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/8c/8c70b499e599314c309470a3a46b99ab3172774886aa08c5dccffac0f130721b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "c5097abf-c541-5c52-85d3-1e56a4e53cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5591" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](images/9e/9e09cbd3d953c8ce24e0a4b8a2b37c90dc3fc00d20c008b64dd068737c6ff49e.svg)\n\nThe least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/bb/bb44b493b7c78eb144467289be8a7eef6f56c65ae37efcd742475c1ac0a55690.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "8a39f109-9436-55f8-bbb8-87253668378d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5963" :question/problem "![](images/71/7122f0c4dd74925137e301a4b0ac3672d1f1b30cadf19f3bce438bce202b5720.svg)\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as \n![](images/d4/d48e14c19cb4927af5d1199fe7a8f34ff4ddd96314f56d69534d438786dc2ff9.svg)\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f6d487-6ec9-51e0-b88b-fc9df19cbc3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367431} {:answer/id #uuid "03cb6b05-17f1-5921-96f3-cc50808a26ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367432} {:answer/id #uuid "9d682f1d-3ed3-5788-8567-22a0ee51538d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367433} {:answer/id #uuid "7825acfd-a465-5c06-8fd7-d1224330d940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367434} {:answer/id #uuid "3e78bee4-76bc-5377-abfd-3838c1bbc207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7825acfd-a465-5c06-8fd7-d1224330d940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367434} :answer-field/id #uuid "a655004e-1ad8-59c2-9d78-74eb309d05be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcaaa14-b976-59cf-b555-9129a6aa2fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-136821" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -17 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & -17 \\\\ -17 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=17+(-17)+15=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053e502-fa88-53f4-be0b-479818f8c240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442290} {:answer/id #uuid "299f4294-b751-5683-b5f0-226953e8f833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442291} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3bc517-738d-5126-88d0-bb939758899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442292} {:answer/id #uuid "65d7077d-9a54-50ce-b898-454bc4952add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442293} {:answer/id #uuid "95715e13-7ac3-5517-874f-49e84cbcd24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95715e13-7ac3-5517-874f-49e84cbcd24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442294} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b40939-3724-59f7-a9d3-46896f75c235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9375f3f0-3aa6-5a84-b715-8086fa3c68db" :question/math-academy-id "q-136883" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & a & b \\\\ 5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 20 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=20+(-10)+(-6)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20388d9f-ef78-593d-a86d-57d7b9255118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424008} {:answer/id #uuid "6235969c-cd1d-5e61-9e7a-9328ddcaeab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424009} {:answer/id #uuid "75f256ad-47a3-5c49-a78e-1c5ae0257228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424010} {:answer/id #uuid "903e90ec-6c0f-5989-8ade-87db319935c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424012} {:answer/id #uuid "adae751b-b0cd-56eb-9595-ed2b595298d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20388d9f-ef78-593d-a86d-57d7b9255118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424008} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8116051-5575-52c2-b4e3-895e85818216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "130f7ccc-d087-5052-915e-6df794e733bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-136921" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+4=54.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "612c1237-1d75-5779-901e-394b60ae8abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415123} {:answer/id #uuid "985ff754-4000-553d-84fd-3adae2a90775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415127} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f160c1-cc03-5355-b516-d2d19b494551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415129} {:answer/id #uuid "b6774fe7-0b94-51e6-8366-d780b5d12442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415130} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32f448-8a3a-522b-9c2c-5ad76f739b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985ff754-4000-553d-84fd-3adae2a90775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415127} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf2aa749-d040-57c2-b657-f15f0f4f8d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "090045f3-969e-5b87-b1e6-6f48922501d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-136945" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+3b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=-2,\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+3b+c=0+3\\cdot (-2)+1=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5a9c23-8d4c-5a61-af41-8b6d2c96bcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266659} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fa2a26-e812-549e-8835-6295aa5319d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267747} {:answer/id #uuid "cf723ad7-5492-59ea-aec1-79f455aab318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267999} {:answer/id #uuid "db4cc96a-2085-57a1-9317-5c474924f1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268084} {:answer/id #uuid "f40e0d44-f7ca-5e19-a8b9-8693564691ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf723ad7-5492-59ea-aec1-79f455aab318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267999} :answer-field/id #uuid "d11dceef-fa44-5f21-b07d-72b4a644c05f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef71613a-c794-54f5-9a74-68efbe48ffea" :question/math-academy-id "q-137034" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1c90f3-c1b6-573f-ab6c-07ec11bf9f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266365} {:answer/id #uuid "4aab64f6-7242-5884-9cd4-b0a3a4a9143e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266805} {:answer/id #uuid "5bba4d55-7c36-5233-8c45-c58d4705d39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266956} {:answer/id #uuid "90976ab1-62e5-54ff-a6c5-42bb9d3b1bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267431} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4dfd78-93dd-5e18-9a5e-04b304c3c8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a1c90f3-c1b6-573f-ab6c-07ec11bf9f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266365} :answer-field/id #uuid "5364be24-88fb-5a2f-b7c6-4e394496b06e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26d67fbf-4de7-566c-9152-9d1736d17888" :question/math-academy-id "q-137061" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e85101-f9b2-5d74-83c6-aabdd8fc2745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415114} {:answer/id #uuid "2baff1db-5ead-50b2-af5a-abe277ae4820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415115} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccd28ea-578f-5414-8332-a6408e6df178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415116} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8878bb-5944-584d-9b48-2add56ecc5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415117} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd78f8e-e662-5c98-aa4d-0c408cd252ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd78f8e-e662-5c98-aa4d-0c408cd252ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415126} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27c023d-32d0-5cf3-ade4-1f1674089f41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a92698e-17e4-57b8-8191-7aa9adc0e63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-137092" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{4b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{4b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71872f14-7cc4-5fb9-8cf7-9ffe96aefe19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267142} {:answer/id #uuid "725897a0-9e77-57f9-b8a9-68f56715093b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267146} {:answer/id #uuid "828574ad-8b28-58c3-ac93-daffbb878a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267291} {:answer/id #uuid "9d160852-1af1-56d1-8d23-a808bbfc6b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267556} {:answer/id #uuid "edbdca09-407a-5b44-b814-062fce5150c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "828574ad-8b28-58c3-ac93-daffbb878a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267291} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b80840-f020-56db-8303-eb251d19e950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb576d6c-dcbf-591c-a5dd-97e97349a8f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-137095" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ -14 & 14 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4a+\\frac{b}{c}=4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b66e8f9-798c-5fb5-9702-3848b450ff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266376} {:answer/id #uuid "1c88a6e9-e50e-5742-b36a-eb35a929b299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266387} {:answer/id #uuid "275bb128-5a9f-5d1f-abdd-0647ca8896e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266494} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed0a256-a969-57ba-a38b-d639f21e2e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266980} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3b665d-ddd7-5f8f-a302-3a3d75a9b19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ed0a256-a969-57ba-a38b-d639f21e2e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266980} :answer-field/id #uuid "343b4b87-3893-598e-8c73-4fe5b3ba88f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93179ecb-11e4-5253-ab06-7702070386da" :question/math-academy-id "q-137097" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 27\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ -27 & 27 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-9a+\\frac{b}{c}=-9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{1}{1}=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c10002-500f-5b1c-9ac3-3bccf7a34be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266786} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9f182d-98f4-54f1-b0c2-ab6dea49e81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267087} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9ed506-cab1-5977-a1b0-8323058b9941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267114} {:answer/id #uuid "7aae27b3-24bc-58a7-8eb4-69dda6ad9f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267226} {:answer/id #uuid "b759c551-e5e4-5b83-9eba-bf5f0616ed57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aae27b3-24bc-58a7-8eb4-69dda6ad9f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267226} :answer-field/id #uuid "9274ef15-b4ba-50d9-b139-ab18e30c28bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58ba77df-63a2-5a9b-ba59-e2f2dbd16055" :question/math-academy-id "q-137119" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $7a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ -28 & 56 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n7a+\\frac{b}{c}=7\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{2}{1}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10eb91a6-2d3a-53b3-b073-a10d66decb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324127} {:answer/id #uuid "398c42e6-61f6-5edb-a6af-56df8a41a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324439} {:answer/id #uuid "70e7b5cd-ce67-5487-8c59-0136c6664fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324901} {:answer/id #uuid "73b39860-f650-5a09-8af5-6cb9c76b62c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324931} {:answer/id #uuid "9fba6c6f-ee94-52be-9456-59da4a7eee4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398c42e6-61f6-5edb-a6af-56df8a41a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324439} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1a7497-9e7d-5e11-87dd-d6334e26484a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3df77ba-fc93-5eb8-9827-0c8cef3d9027" :question/math-academy-id "q-40923" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-27 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ -30 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}=-10\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a2e5c9-1ec3-53d7-9d65-67e652469f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324010} {:answer/id #uuid "48074d8b-44cf-5ab6-976d-e49c216938d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324554} {:answer/id #uuid "5dad58d2-2933-50ed-bf94-504e98ebf78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324749} {:answer/id #uuid "c15de438-95e3-56a6-9a64-f99e256f9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325633} {:answer/id #uuid "e20db4f3-7b86-5a65-991a-7ed79cec5074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dad58d2-2933-50ed-bf94-504e98ebf78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324749} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ca8095-e929-5df1-909d-a6fa762d4c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6fc54b-cb36-5749-9df6-d5883bd36fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40927" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-2a+\\frac{b}{c}=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c8cf07-1252-5e91-b5c9-12f38c1f946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324034} {:answer/id #uuid "91384dd4-5bcc-5860-814c-f96556460493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325204} {:answer/id #uuid "93d14029-007f-54bd-9d2a-0c82c9066807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325227} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f7e58b-5b63-5e1f-ae19-6232045969c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325419} {:answer/id #uuid "b3759c15-e036-5c30-848d-c2b42024912f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c8cf07-1252-5e91-b5c9-12f38c1f946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324034} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314ea6d-cf55-58df-9abc-6434d47d5c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db567127-1e8b-50cb-a3d2-dc5e2ea462cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-40950" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcce621-ad6c-5e38-92ce-59257d82b2e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324207} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae637f1-21bd-58c0-8588-1f56b6ddf620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324445} {:answer/id #uuid "4f41b66f-c2b8-5e09-b24b-82987c5cd64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324621} {:answer/id #uuid "7508b388-8184-5a28-8894-9eb4c9ab46c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324944} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac2159-63e1-59da-a0e5-00c6faec0140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f41b66f-c2b8-5e09-b24b-82987c5cd64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324621} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdd0b61-b5b9-5f31-8491-d5721c2ec5db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80e97aa1-dac8-53af-afe4-09e324e932eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-41909" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=5 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}245 & 70 & a \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ c & 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}245 & 70 & 35 \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 35 & 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=35+10+35=80.\n$$"}]}
